  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How do you picture complex numbers?",
      "a": "On the Argand diagram: the real part goes across and the imaginary part goes up. The number 3 plus 2i is the point 3 across and 2 up.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an Argand diagram?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does multiplying by i do?",
      "a": "It rotates the point a quarter turn, 90 degrees anticlockwise, about zero. For example, 1 goes to i, and 2 plus i goes to minus 1 plus 2i.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does i rotate numbers?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is i squared minus 1?",
      "a": "Multiplying by i twice is two quarter turns, which is a half turn. A half turn takes 1 to minus 1. So i times i is minus 1.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does i^2 = -1?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is i to the power 4?",
      "a": "Four quarter turns make a full turn, back to the start, so i to the power 4 is 1.",
      "asks": [
        "What is i^4?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Are imaginary numbers real?",
      "a": "They are as real as negative numbers once were. They describe real things like rotations, waves and electric currents, and engineers use them every day.",
      "asks": [
        "Do imaginary numbers exist?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "i is defined so that i² = −1.",
    "Multiplying a complex number by i rotates it 90° anticlockwise about the origin on the Argand diagram."
